--TEST--
`Core\Json::encode` refuses a `secret` anywhere in the value it walks, an `array<secret string>` included — ADR 0033 § 4's serialiser sink
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0033 § 4's serialiser bullet: an encoded document is on its way to a
// response, a log or a queue, and none of those is the credential being
// *used*. It is a call-site rule for the debug sink's reason — `encode`
// declares `mixed`, which a `secret string` satisfies, so the written
// argument is the last place the qualifier is visible at all.
string $plain = "p";
tainted string $t = "t";
secret string $s = "s";
secret bytes $sb = "s" as bytes;
array<secret string> $bag = ["token" => $s];

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. `tainted` is not on this list: ADR 0024
// § 4 is about injection, and an encoder is not one of its sinks.
echo Core\Json::encode($plain), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode($t), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(["a", "b"]), "\n";

// The bare argument, on both bases.
Core\Json::encode($s);
Core\Json::encode($sb);

// The value is *walked*: refusing only a bare `secret` argument would leave
// the shape the rule exists for — one credential among public fields —
// open. A written `["token" => $s]` is not this: an array literal with no
// expectation infers `array<mixed>`, so the qualifier is erased before the
// call sees it, which is ADR 0033's own unmodelled container axis rather
// than a hole in this rule.
Core\Json::encode($bag);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0791]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot be passed to `Core\Json::encode`; an encoded document is on its way to a response, a log or a queue, and none of those is the credential being used
   --> case.nvs:21:19
